Prove that KERI-family implementations agree = goal:
  nid: 26kmlhsh
  stage-status: in-progress
  why: >-
    Implementations of CESR, KERI, ACDC and IPEX outside keripy have no way to show they are correct; a 2026-09 survey of ten Rust codebases found the same defects (for example, CESR 2.0 counters that count items where keripy counts quadlets) that a shared corpus would have caught on day one. Chose a standalone suite of fixed cases with implementation-neutral expected verdicts over adding tests to each spec or each implementation, because the interesting cases cross specs (a KEL case is also a CESR case) and test data churns at a different rate from spec prose. Tradeoff: a separate artifact to govern and keep in step with the specs, and a risk of enshrining keripy's behaviour as law, which the case-provenance and disputed-case rules exist to contain.
  children:
    The spec text is the authority, not keripy = constraint:
      nid: 9vnp6fz7
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        keripy generates most expected values, so without a counterweight its bugs become law and the suite certifies sameness-with-keripy rather than conformance. Chose spec authority with keripy as a provisional generator: a case whose expected value an implementation's behaviour contradicts, where the cited clause appears to say otherwise, is marked disputed, excluded from conformance tallies, and taken to the spec working group; the suite never resolves the dispute itself. Revised 2026-10-02: conflicts inside the spec's own text no longer make a case disputed (see the keyword-sentence decision). Rejected treating keripy as the reference outright. Tradeoff: disputed cases sit unresolved for as long as the working group takes.
    No case without a normative clause = decision:
      nid: 8v0gc5w4
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        Every assertion in a case cites the spec clause it tests and carries that clause's requirement level; MUST assertions decide conformance and SHOULD and MAY results are reported separately. Revised 2026-10-01 after the keri-review-panel (KRT-F3): the level was first attached to whole cases, which is wrong because one case checks several things at different levels (an event under its signing threshold checks a MUST not-accepted-on-arrival and a SHOULD accepted-once-completed, since the KERI spec says only that such an event SHOULD be escrowed). Corrected 2026-10-05: this node first gave out-of-order delivery as the example, but the spec's only SHOULD-escrow (KERI spec v1.0.1 L1266) covers signature and witness threshold shortfalls; out-of-order and delegation escrow have no text and are graded only in a named profile. Behaviour no clause requires (escrow kinds, error taxonomies, HTTP endpoints, the CESR 1.00 count-code table, roles without a spec) goes into a labelled non-normative profile. Rejected testing keripy behaviour wholesale because it would make the suite the de facto spec. Tradeoff: some genuinely useful interop checks are demoted to non-normative profiles, and case files are larger.
    Coarse, normative verdict vocabulary = decision:
      nid: 4mc8vyhg
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        KERI dispositions are decided by an ordered procedure, each step citing its clause: an intrinsic failure (encoding, SAID, schema, d not equal to i), which nothing can cure; then no verifiable controller signature, judged against key state the validator holds (MUST drop, never escrow, L1266); then a constraint of state the validator holds that forbids the event (establishment-only, do-not-delegate, non-transferable, abandoned), with the same state-held guard; then not yet acceptable relative to the trunk (threshold shortfall, prior event not seen, prior digest or pre-rotation commitment not matching the trunk, delegating seal not seen), which later events or a supersession can cure; then a conflict on the trunk (seen if rules A or B permit it to supersede, else discarded, MUST for a rotation by C1 L1823); else seen. Each message is read as seen or not, at quiescence after its own delivery and after the last, and as on the trunk or not at the end, which the adapter must drive; refinements of not-seen (pending, rejected) are graded only where a clause states them, and duplicitous is informative for a validator. Revised 2026-10-01 after the keri-review-panel (KRT-F1, KRT-F2, SPC-F3, SEC-F2, SEC-F7): the first version had four overlapping dispositions with no precedence, no value for a superseded event, no defined read point, and no stated validator perspective; cases now state a perspective. Revised 2026-10-05 after the keri-catalogue panel (KRT-F2, KRT-F5, KRT-F6, SKP-F5): the accepted/not-accepted bit became the seen and trunk readings, the state-constraint step and the intrinsic/relative split were added, and receipts get dispositions only where a clause grades them. Rejected keripy's escrow taxonomy as normative, and rejected letting keripy decide dispositions, because the suite must be able to find keripy wrong. Tradeoff: generators must compute dispositions from the procedure rather than read them off keripy.
    Published expectations are immutable = decision:
      nid: 9ppgdqn5
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        A case id is permanent and its expected verdict never changes; a correction is a new case id with the old case deprecated and pointing to it. This is what keeps the suite maintainable for implementers: a result recorded against suite version X never silently flips meaning, and a regression is always a code change on their side or a new case on ours. Rejected in-place correction, which is cheaper for us and turns every suite release into an unexplained diff for every implementation. Tradeoff: deprecated cases accumulate and ids are not contiguous by topic.
    Fixtures are generated, never hand-edited = decision:
      nid: 4204xnkm
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        Cases are produced from small declarative scenario files by generators, and CI regenerates every fixture in a pinned environment and fails on any byte difference. This makes the anti-pattern the survey and the craftsman ledger both warn about, editing an expectation to make an implementation pass, impossible to merge rather than merely forbidden. Rejected committing hand-curated fixtures, which is how cesr-test-vectors stalled with its streams section still TBD. Tradeoff: every new case needs generator support first, and the generators become code we must maintain at the bar of the runner.
    Target versions are wire versions = decision:
      nid: 9fls7thp
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        Case inputs are identified by the protocol versions on the wire, not by keripy release numbers, because keripy's 2.x line implements version 1.0 of most specs while emitting 2.00 codes. A profile's status comes from the maturity of the text its assertions cite, not from wire versions. Revised 2026-10-01 after the keri-review-panel (SKP-F3): the first version made every 2.x case draft and advisory, which would have demoted the count-code defect that motivated the suite even though CESR spec v1.0 states it as a MUST. Expected values from a moving implementation (keripy main ahead of a release) are draft and pinned to a commit; behaviour with no normative text, such as keripy 1.x's CESR 1.00 count codes, goes into a profile named for interoperability with keripy 1.x. Tradeoff: draft cases need regeneration as keripy moves, producing new ids when expectations change.
    Adapters speak JSON lines over stdio = decision:
      nid: 0qxf76ss
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        An implementation is connected through one long-lived adapter process that reads one JSON request per line and writes one JSON response per line, starting with a hello handshake that declares the adapter protocol version, the implementation's identity and commit, and its supported features. Chose stdio JSON lines over a language binding or HTTP because it works for any language, needs no port and no network, and makes an adapter a small standalone program. The runner never sends a case that needs an undeclared feature, so not-supported is decided by the declaration and an adapter cannot use it to hide a failure. Tradeoff: process startup per run and JSON escaping of binary CESR, which travels hex-encoded.
    Adapters work without upstream acceptance = decision:
      nid: 93t4d880
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        Each adapter builds against a released or pinned version of its implementation using only that implementation's public API, never patches it, and composes any missing entry point on top, as the survey's host layer did for devrandom. CI builds every adapter against its pin. Chose this because upstream acceptance is uncertain (each project has its own licence, governance and priorities, and may reasonably decline to host an adapter) and the suite must not depend on it. Tradeoff: adapters carry glue code that an upstream project could have exposed more cleanly, and they can lag behind implementation API changes.
    The keripy adapter stays in the suite = decision:
      nid: 5x86gnx3
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        keripy is both a generator and an implementation under test, so its adapter is maintained here permanently rather than offered upstream. Every result where keripy produced the expected value and keripy is also the implementation under test is flagged self-agreement, because a defect shared by generator and implementation passes silently; the flag is the witness-qualifier keripy-tests-keripy blind spot carried over. Tradeoff: we own an adapter for a moving codebase we do not control.
    Cases that disclose a vulnerability are embargoed = decision:
      nid: 5cz9hck0
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        A case that a released implementation, keripy included, handles with a security consequence (accepts what should be rejected, retains what should be dropped, lets one input disturb the processing of others, or crashes or hangs) is a public proof of concept against it, whatever the level of the assertion. Such a case is held in a private repository, with its scenario, baseline changes and nightly results, until the project has been told privately and has had up to 90 days to ship a fix. The embargo outranks publishing a keripy-contradicting case as disputed. Revised 2026-10-01 (SEC-F5) and 2026-10-02: the trigger was first tied to failing a MUST, but a case can move to SHOULD under the inferred-obligation rule while its security consequence is unchanged; the consequence decides, not the grade. Tradeoff: the public suite lags what we know, and a private CI path must exist.
      children:
        The embargo list's location is configured, not assumed = decision:
          nid: 6q64urzv
          why: >-
            The pre-push guard (tools/embargo_guard.py) used to find the embargo list by assuming one maintainer's checkout layout, a private repository beside this one, which bound a public tool to a directory structure nobody else has and named a repository outsiders cannot see. It now takes the list from --patterns, then the KCS_EMBARGO_PATTERNS environment variable, then git config kcs.embargoPatterns (a relative value resolves against the main checkout, so every worktree agrees), and otherwise looks in the git directory at info/embargo-patterns.txt, which no commit can carry and every worktree shares. A list that was configured and cannot be read refuses the push, and so does a setting that is present but empty, because a maintainer who configured one holds embargoed material and a typo in the setting must not silently switch the check off; only an unconfigured default where nothing exists at all, the normal state for a contributor without a list, warns and lets the push through, while anything present there (a dangling symlink, a directory) must be readable or refuses. Rejected keeping the old sibling path as a fallback, which keeps the private name in the public tool. Rejected making a missing list always fatal, which would block every contributor who holds none. Tradeoff: an existing maintainer clone must set the location once, and until it does its pushes are unchecked with a warning.
    Bakobo methodology is incubation-only = decision:
      nid: 3l9vqm6q
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        this.i, tick and the Bakobo standards block are Bakobo's practice, not the KERI Foundation's, and will be removed at the handoff (Daniel, 2026-10-01). So every rule a future maintainer needs is also written as prose under docs/, and this.i may explain why but is never the only home of a rule. Rejected keeping the reasoning only in this.i, which would leave the Foundation with rules and no rationale after removal. Tradeoff: some reasoning is stated twice and can drift; docs/ wins if they disagree after handoff.
    Apache-2.0 for test vectors, not CC-BY-4.0 = deviation:
      nid: 4g6579k6
      stage-status: done
      deviates-from: bakobo/dev standards/oss-posture.md item 1 (CC-BY-4.0 for corpora)
      scope: cases/, scenarios/ and profiles/ in this repository
      approved-by: Daniel Hardman, 2026-10-01
      why: >-
        The posture standard licenses corpora under CC-BY-4.0, but these vectors are test fixtures that implementations copy into their own Apache- and MIT-licensed test trees; a second licence on the fixtures would add attribution obligations and a licence-compatibility question to every adopter for no gain. Approved by Daniel, 2026-10-01.
    Anything but an answer is a failure = decision:
      nid: 7j5hvp2v
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        For every case the runner sends, a crash, a hang past the time limit, an oversized or malformed response, or an error reply fails every assertion in the case; only runner-side faults, such as failing to start the adapter, go unscored. Chosen after the keri-review-panel (SEC-F4): routing these outcomes to a neutral harness bucket would let a parser that panics or loops on hostile input escape exactly the must-reject cases built for it, the defect class the survey found. Rejected distinguishing adapter-glue exceptions from implementation exceptions, because the runner cannot see the difference and an adapter could launder failures through it. Tradeoff: a buggy adapter makes its implementation look worse than it is, which pushes adapter quality onto adapter authors.
    Adapters declare the behaviour they compose = decision:
      nid: 0eqxk4w6
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        When an adapter supplies logic its implementation lacks (escrow, routing, duplicity detection on top of a verification library), it lists that behaviour in the handshake's composes field, and every result record and conformance claim carries the list. Chosen after the keri-review-panel (SEC-F6): otherwise a claim such as passes every MUST case would credit a bare library with refusing duplicitous state that only the adapter refused. Rejected forbidding composition, which would exclude verification-only libraries such as devrandom's from the KERI layer entirely. Tradeoff: claims become tuples that are harder to summarize.
    IPEX transition cases wait for normative text = decision:
      nid: 69e5z0wq
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        The suite tests IPEX exchange messages for well-formedness, signatures and references now, and adds transcript cases classifying valid and invalid next steps only when the specs state a normative transition rule. Chosen after the keri-review-panel (KRT-F5): no clause defines which message may follow which or when a message opens a new exchange, keripy's own handlers disagree, and the first worked example would have failed a route-table-faithful implementation. Rejected deriving the state machine from keripy, which would make the suite the IPEX spec. Tradeoff: contractually protected disclosure, a core motivation, stays untested at the protocol level until the working group acts.
      children:
        IPEX messages are graded by KERI's exn rules = decision:
          nid: 6hx8msj8
          why: >-
            The ACDC spec's IPEX section declares itself non-normative (line 1797) even though it contains keyword sentences, and the separate IPEX draft is informational. The only normative text for an IPEX message is KERI v1.0.1's exchange-message rules: the exn field set (line 1197), the prior-message link p (975) and the exchange id x, which must be the SAID of an xip exchange-inception message (979). So IPEX cases are exn cases graded against those clauses through an exn.verify adapter operation, and the ACDC an IPEX grant carries is graded by acdc.verify. An implementation that starts exchanges without xip is disputed against KERI 979, not excused. Daniel agreed 2026-10-09 (Q-VQZ0). Rejected citing the non-normative IPEX section's sentences, which a section that disclaims normativity cannot support. Tradeoff: IPEX cases are thin until the working group makes IPEX normative. Revised 2026-10-09 after the acdc-design keri-review-panel (SKP-F6, SEC-F6, SEC-F7; SKP-F3 declined): every clause these cases cite is KERI's, so exchange-message cases belong to the KERI layer and the keri-v1.0.1 profile, under KERI case ids, which gives a KERI-only implementation exn coverage and stops an acdc-v1.0 result implying IPEX was tested; the IPEX layer holds only cases resting on IPEX text, of which there are none until IPEX is normative. A lone exn with an empty p and a non-empty x stays graded, because it is refused under either reading of its transaction membership: outside a transaction KERI 979 requires x to be empty, and inside one it requires an xip first. A sender-binding case, another AID's valid signature on an exn whose i is the sender, is added at MUST (KERI 1266, 1737). Revised again 2026-10-09 after the acdc-exn-cases keri-review-panel (SEC-F1, SKP-F1, KRT-F4): the exchange-message cases use a neutral route (/kcs/exchange) and an empty payload rather than IPEX routes and ACDCs, because every clause they cite is about any exchange message and a deployment with IPEX route handlers would refuse the positive pairs for IPEX reasons, leaving the refusals passable by a validator that refuses everything; the batch includes multi-key senders, numeric and weighted, whose refusals carry at least one valid sender signature, because with single-key senders the line-1266 floor alone produced every MUST and the line-1260 threshold went untested; and transaction membership is by x (979: x "universally uniquely associates" a message with its transaction), so an x naming a held xip with a p from another transaction is a 975 prior failure, while 979's x rule is cited only for an x naming a held message that is not an xip, which breaks it under either reading.
          children:
            An exchange-message reading describes a delivery = decision:
              nid: 8r78y7st
              why: >-
                exn.verify reads each exchange message twice, on its own delivery and after the last, and the protocol did not say whether a reading is about the message body or about one delivery of it. Keyed by body, a forged copy delivered after the genuine message (same body, signatures by another key) reads accepted on the genuine copy's strength, which the hostile review of PR #20 reproduced. Chose: a reading describes a delivery, the message together with the attachments it arrived with, and a second delivery of the same body is read on its own, so an adapter reports what its implementation did with that delivery's own attachments. Decided by Claude 2026-10-09 while Daniel was away (D-YNZF, resolving tick 45bv); easy to reverse before any exchange-message case exists. Rejected per-body readings, which let one valid delivery vouch for any later copy. Tradeoff: an adapter must observe per-delivery outcomes, which for keripy means wrapping its Exchanger's processing rather than reading its database alone.
    The runner has no runtime dependencies = decision:
      nid: 4fmwzdjs
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        kcs is a Python 3.12+ package managed with uv and depends on nothing at runtime. It must install wherever an adapter author works, in whatever language, and must never pull in an implementation under test, which depending on keripy or a CESR library would do. Generators that drive keripy run in their own pinned environments. Rejected writing the runner in Rust or Go for a single static binary: the generators and the first adapter are Python anyway, and a static binary would still need a release pipeline before anyone could use it. Tradeoff: adapter authors need a Python 3.12 interpreter on the machine that runs the suite. Recorded after the 2026-10-01 design review (CON-F1); the decision was made in commit cdf9ec0 without a node.
    Adapters are checkable without running cases = decision:
      nid: 8jdu4g9r
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        The adapter protocol is a contract implemented outside this repo in several languages, so the suite ships JSON Schemas for every protocol message and a kcs check-adapter command that probes handshake validity, id echo, error handling, statelessness between requests and escrow quiescence; the runner also runs the statelessness probe each session and can compare a shuffled run with an ordered one. Chosen after the 2026-10-01 design review (ARC-F1, TST-F1, TST-F2): without probes, an adapter that leaks state or skips quiescence produces wrong results the runner cannot see, and the org's contract standard asks a provider to ship vectors consumers run. Rejected static request/response vectors alone, because the invariants that matter are behavioural. Tradeoff: the probes are code to maintain, and they can only detect, not prove, compliance.
    The runner contains the adapter = decision:
      nid: 09wtltes
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        The runner treats adapters as untrusted: response size and request time bounds, a capped line reader, process-group kill, and on POSIX memory and CPU rlimits, a scrubbed environment and a refusal to run as root; network isolation is delegated to CI jobs that hold no secrets. Chosen after the 2026-10-01 design review (SEC-F1, SEC-F2): hostile inputs are the suite's normal payload, and an allocation bomb or parser exploit must not take down the host or reach credentials. Rejected requiring a container or sandbox runtime, which would make the suite unusable on developer laptops. Tradeoff: on non-POSIX platforms the runner offers weaker containment, and the docs say so.
    Keyword sentences govern conflicts inside a spec = decision:
      nid: 740f56ur
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        Where a sentence with an RFC 2119 keyword conflicts with a table, legend or example in the same specification, the assertion keeps the sentence's level, stays active, records the conflict in spec_conflicts and raises it with the working group. Decided by Daniel 2026-10-02 (option 2 of three) after the CESR catalogue review found the count-code rule at CESR spec line 591 contradicted by its own symbol legends. Rejected treating such conflicts as disputed, which would have excluded the founding case and about 20 others and made the suite say almost nothing until the spec is fixed. Tradeoff: the suite adopts one interpretive rule, which a spec author could contest.
    Inferred validator obligations are graded SHOULD = decision:
      nid: 20d9xrm2
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        Where a specification states an obligation only for producers (pad bits zero, counts matching content) and the suite infers what a validator should do with a violation, the assertion is graded SHOULD with the inference recorded in inferred_from, and the gap is raised as a request that the specification state the validator rule; a stated rule later re-issues the case at its level under a new id. Decided by Daniel 2026-10-02. Rejected grading inferences MUST, which legislates on no text and invites the charge that the suite invents the specification; rejected moving them out of conformance entirely, which hides the security-relevant rejection cases. Tradeoff: a lenient parser can claim MUST conformance while failing visible SHOULDs.
    Item extents are a declared capability; summaries answer the rest = decision:
      nid: 909lzg4n
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        Some CESR libraries accept a stream without exposing where each item starts and ends (Affinidi's parser; parts of cesrox and keripy 1.2.14), and the integrity rule forbids an adapter computing extents from counts, so before this they could only answer unsupported and gave no evidence even on must-reject cases, which need no extents. Chose a cesr.item-extents feature, required by every case with a decoded assertion and declared only by an adapter that reports items for every stream it accepts, plus an accepted summary {consumed} that a rejected assertion can be graded against. Rejected relaxing the integrity rule, which would test adapter arithmetic instead of the library, and rejected per-group-code features, which would multiply the vocabulary for gaps no case exercises. Tradeoff: an adapter with any itemization gap gives up all decoded evidence, so keriox and the keripy 1.2.14 build report decoded interop cases as not-supported.
    KERI MUST conformance means safety = decision:
      nid: 08679vxn
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        In the KERI layer a MUST assertion tests safety: the validator never accepts what the specification forbids and drops what it says to drop. That includes the superseding-recovery limits, because C1 (KERI spec v1.0.1 L1823) says the root KEL MUST be non-delegated, therefore A or B MUST be satisfied, or else the superseding rotation MUST be discarded, and a non-delegated KEL is its own root; so a rotation signed with a stolen current key that tries to supersede outside rules A and B is refused at MUST for every KEL. Accepting valid events (liveness) stays SHOULD, inferred, because no sentence obliges a validator to accept anything (L1780 and L1258-L1264 are preconditions). Decided by Daniel 2026-10-05 after the keri-catalogue review panel (SEC-F1, SKP-F2, SKP-F3) found the first framing graded the pre-rotation firewall and the retrograde defence at SHOULD, so a validator accepting a thief's rot1' passed every MUST. Rejected grounding refusal in L178's one-valid-KEL rule, which constrains how many KELs exist and not which event belongs on the trunk (KRT-F1). Rejected grading liveness MUST from L53, which is about trust, not acceptance (KRT-F4). Tradeoff: a validator that accepts nothing passes every MUST, which the SHOULD-reporting rule on claims exists to expose; and a conflicting interaction is outside C1's text, so its refusal is SHOULD until the spec keywords it.
    KERI dispositions are two readings, seen and trunk = decision:
      nid: 3pl4r69q
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        Each delivered message is reported by two readings: whether it is seen (accepted into the validator's KEL, which the spec says is permanent: first seen, always seen, never unseen, L1788), read at quiescence after its own delivery and after the last message; and whether it is on the trunk at the end. Superseded is seen and off the trunk (L1799: superseded events still remain in the KEL). Each reading is graded at the level of the clause that governs it. Chosen after the keri-catalogue review panel (KRT-F2): the single accepted bit conflated the monotone seen relation with the non-monotone trunk relation, so a recovery case had no defined final value and a conflict check could be read against either relation. Rejected keeping superseded as a third final value, which refines neither reading and cannot be graded at a different level from the seen half. Tradeoff: every adapter reports one more field per message, and an implementation without recovery must still report trunk (equal to seen).
    KERI key-state assertions are conditional = decision:
      nid: 7zdhxys3
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        A key-state assertion names one message and applies only if that message was finally seen: if the event was accepted, the key state is exactly this. It keeps the level of the clause that fixes the state's content, and when the condition does not hold its outcome is not-applicable, neither pass nor fail. Chosen after the keri-catalogue review panel (KRT-F3): the earlier rule graded a key-state assertion at the weakest level of the dispositions it depended on, and since every acceptance is SHOULD, the safety content of key state (a broken-commitment rotation must not change the keys) was demoted to SHOULD; an implementation that refused the rotation but applied it to its state passed every MUST. Rejected the weakest-level rule (removed from the design). Tradeoff: a validator that accepts nothing makes every key-state assertion not-applicable, which the SHOULD-reporting rule on claims exposes.
    Conformance claims report their SHOULD results = decision:
      nid: 8pz99y5p
      stage-status: planned
      why: >-
        The runner's report and printed summary carry the counts of active SHOULD assertions by outcome beside the MUST verdict, and say explicitly when any failed; a conformance claim states them too. Chosen after the keri-catalogue review panel (SKP-F3): with liveness at SHOULD and key state conditional, a validator that accepts nothing passes every KERI MUST assertion, and a bare MUST claim from it would read the same as a working validator's. Rejected raising liveness to MUST, which no keyword sentence supports, and rejected a separate liveness verdict, which would need a liveness tag the case schema does not carry. Tradeoff: the claim is longer, and the runner cannot tell a failed liveness SHOULD from a failed inferred-rejection SHOULD, so it reports both together.
    KERI case bytes come from the specification = decision:
      nid: 0mhgn74x
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        The KERI generator builds event bodies from the specification's field rules (field order, version string L265, SAID by the CESR SAID protocol) and signs them with a standard-library Ed25519 checked against RFC 8032 and a BLAKE3 checked against its official vectors; keripy main, in a pinned side environment, cross-checks by rebuilding every untampered body byte for byte, verifying every signature and replaying each case, and a disagreement is recorded in generators/DISAGREEMENTS.md without changing an expected value. Chosen because regenerate --check runs in CI without keripy, and because bytes keripy produced could not show keripy wrong about bytes. Rejected driving keripy's builders, the first plan, for that reason. Tradeoff: the suite carries its own Ed25519 and BLAKE3, which are slow and must never be used outside generation.
    KERI expectations are checked under three keep policies = decision:
      nid: 6wvyl5ft
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        The generator runs a model validator, the design's decision procedure steps 1 to 6, over the delivered bytes three times: keeping every not-yet-acceptable event, keeping only threshold shortfalls (the one SHOULD-escrow, L1266), and keeping nothing. Each scenario names the step it expects each message to reach, and a mismatch is a generation error; an assertion whose expected value would differ between policies is refused, because the specification lets a validator choose its policy and a MUST cannot depend on that choice. Rejected stating expected readings directly in scenarios, which would let a hand error become a published expectation. Tradeoff: some useful checks cannot be written, because they hold only under one policy; those go to the keri-escrow profile at INTEROP.
    KERI key-state MUST survives a feature gate = decision:
      nid: 2zkmdxm8
      stage-status: done
      why: >-
        In cases gated by keri.escrow or kel.recovery, the conditional key-state assertion keeps its MUST level. Its condition, that a named event was seen, can hold only for an implementation that has the feature, so the gate removes no implementation the assertion could grade, and the ungated safety half of each such scenario is a separate normative case. REVERSED 2026-10-05 by the batch-1 keri-review-panel (SEC-F1, confirmed by SKP, SPC, KRT): the exception was unnecessary and unsafe. A key-state assertion is already conditional on its event being seen, so it is not-applicable to an implementation that never reaches that state; it therefore needs no gate, and gating it instead let an adapter that declares neither keri.escrow nor kel.recovery skip a post-supersession key-state MUST entirely. The exception is removed: a case needing either feature now carries no MUST at all, and its MUSTs, key state included, move to an ungated companion case the scenario names (keri_build.py _companion_case). The rule that a MUST is never gated holds without exception. The earlier rejected alternative, demoting these to SHOULD, is moot now that they survive ungated.
    Clause coverage is measured and published = decision:
      nid: 0xd8xuzx
      why: >-
        A suite that only lists its cases cannot say where it is thin, so neither its maintainers nor an implementer reading a conformance claim can tell a well-tested clause from an untouched one. Chose a committed, rerunnable tool that enumerates every RFC 2119 keyword sentence in each pinned specification, maps every case assertion's clause and inference quotes onto those sentences, and writes a generated per-section report under docs/ that CI checks for staleness, as regenerate --check does for cases. Each sentence also carries a durable, hand-kept triage (testable by a validator, out of scope because it binds a role no adapter plays, not testable with a reason, security-bearing) in a sidecar beside the clause registry, so the report separates gaps from sentences no case could ever cover. The report counts only public cases, so it cannot reveal an embargoed one. Daniel approved a committed tool with a public report over a private one-off (2026-10-09, Q-GF8M). Rejected a one-off audit because it is stale after the next batch of cases, and rejected grading adequacy (edge and boundary depth) mechanically because it needs judgment; that grading goes in a dated narrative assessment that cites the report. Tradeoff: the triage sidecar is judgment that must be kept in step with each new spec pin, and a sentence with no triage shows as unassessed rather than being guessed.
    ACDC cases pin spec v1.0, whose wire is ACDC 2.00 = decision:
      nid: 0t3nvk73
      why: >-
        ACDC cases cite trustoverip/kswg-acdc-specification at tag v1.0 (4a543c5), the released text, as CESR and KERI are pinned on tags. That document normatively defines the ACDC 2.00 wire (version string ACDCMmmGggKKKKSSSS, line 64); for 1.x it requires only that a 2.x implementation accept the old version string (line 68). The 1.x field set, keripy 1.x's SAID computation over the expanded form, and the vcp/iss/rev registry have no normative text anywhere (the PTEL draft has no RFC 2119 keyword). So Daniel's request to build 1.0 and 2.0 cases in parallel (2026-10-09, Q-FRHD) is met by rendering each scenario twice: the 2.00 rendering in the normative profile acdc-v1.0, named for the spec tag as keri-v1.0.1 is (first named acdc-v1.0, after the spec version, until @67nf4ghs renamed the normative profiles for their tags), and the 1.x rendering in the non-normative profile acdc-keripy-1x-interop, as keripy-1x-interop does for CESR 1.00. Bulk-issuance MUSTs exist only on main (f0bd097) and are issued as draft cases against that commit until a tag carries them. Main's worked examples were synced with keripy and are used only as cross-checks, never as expected values. Daniel agreed 2026-10-09 (Q-VQZ0). Rejected pinning main, which would make published cases cite an unreleased text, and rejected normative 1.x cases, which would have no clause to cite. Tradeoff: the profile named acdc-v1.0 tests ACDC 2.00 wire, a naming hazard design.md must state, and SEDI's bulk issuance is tested only as draft until the next tag. Revised 2026-10-09 after the acdc-design keri-review-panel (SPC-F2, CSR-F3, KRT-F7): line 68 is read as a framing obligation, so a 1.x version string is graded only on being parsed and its body framed, and no case grades a valid verdict at MUST on a 1.x version string over a 2.00 field set, a hybrid nobody emits and keripy reads as a 1.0 body. A scenario is rendered in both profiles only when both renderings are decided at the same step with the same verdict and the same facts, so a direct-seal scenario whose 1.x form needs a vcp/iss registry gets a separately named interop case.
    ACDC verdicts are valid, invalid and incomplete; registry state is graded fact = decision:
      nid: 2pcymmuj
      why: >-
        An ACDC case gives a bundle (every KEL involved, registry events, schemas, far-node ACDCs and the presented ACDC in its compact, expanded, partial or selective form) fixed at an evaluation point, and the adapter answers valid, invalid or incomplete, decided by an ordered procedure that mirrors KERI's: intrinsic failures (version string, field order, SAIDs, most-compact SAID), schema, issuer key state, issuance commitment, registry chain, edges, else valid. The registry's verified head state is reported as facts (registry id, sequence number, said, td, ts) and graded as a conditional assertion, like KERI key state. Revoked is not a normative verdict: no clause says a revoked ACDC fails validation, and the spec gives issued and revoked only as example state values (line 2041), so the meaning of a revocation state goes into a non-normative profile. Invalid and incomplete are both not-valid at the level of the clause that failed; the distinction is informative, as design.md already treats undefined refinements. valid is a SHOULD by inference, as accepted is in KERI, because no clause obliges acceptance. Daniel agreed 2026-10-09 (Q-VQZ0). This replaces the four-verdict sketch in design.md (valid, revoked, invalid, incomplete). Rejected a normative revoked verdict, which would make the suite define revocation. Tradeoff: most ACDC safety assertions are inferred SHOULDs until the working group adds validator MUSTs, so an ACDC conformance claim reads weakly. Revised 2026-10-09 after the acdc-design keri-review-panel (KRT-F1, SPC-F1, KRT-F2, KRT-F4, KRT-F5, KRT-F6, SKP-F1, SKP-F2): edge evaluation is full-depth. The bundle holds every ACDC in the presented ACDC's provenance DAG, with their issuers' KELs and registries, bounded in node count and depth, and each edge is reported once, keyed by its near node's SAID and its label path, so multi-hop chains such as vLEI's are renderable and a diamond is unambiguous. An edge is graded on its own checks only (the far node's recomputed SAID equals n, the far node satisfies its own schema and the edge's s, the operator rules where the text says MUST), at MUST and only when the adapter reports the edge; whether an edge is reported is an inferred SHOULD. Propagation of a far node's invalidity to its edge, and of an invalid edge to the near node, is informative in acdc-v1.0, because line 1114 says MAY and defers to the EGF and the NOT operator inverts it; a wholly-valid-chain verdict is graded only in the non-normative profile acdc-chain-strict. This replaces the SHOULD-inferred propagation grade and the rule that read an unreported edge through the verdict. Signature-only issuance leaves the first batch (spec issue A-G3), and every refusal scenario is paired with a positive scenario differing only in the defect, so that a fail-closed stub shows in the liveness results a claim reports. Revised 2026-10-09 after the acdc-exn-cases keri-review-panel (SPC-F1, SEC-F2, SEC-F6, KRT-F1, KRT-F2, KRT-F3, CSR-F1, CSR-F3, SKP-F4): no ACDC assertion cites the non-normative IPEX section, so a direct commitment refusal cites line 95 (the issuer's key state is KERI's) inferred through lines 1663 and 1673, and a refusal through a registry cites line 1669; a seal in an event a recovery rotation superseded commits nothing, and because only a validator that performs recovery can see that, such a case requires kel.recovery as a whole, refusal included, and may carry no MUST, as KERI's KS-64 does; the model never follows the edges of a far node whose SAID fails, which is what makes a forged cycle harmless, and a case presents one; a body framed as another protocol's message is not an ACDC body, which is a different failure from bytes no version string frames, and a version string that declares the wrong size is refused at SHOULD directly (line 62). The first batch is issued draft rather than active, because no implementation has evaluated any ACDC case and its verdicts, registry heads and edge results rest on the suite's model alone; draft cases are run and reported but decide no verdict and cover no clause, and they move to active once an implementation evaluates them or by a recorded decision. Rejected publishing active with permanent ids, which would turn any modelling error into a deprecation. Tradeoff: the batch makes no conformance claim until then.
    ACDC schema verdicts come from a declared JSON Schema subset = decision:
      nid: 6uv8mcxk
      why: >-
        Expected verdicts for schema conformance need a JSON Schema 2020-12 evaluator, and the generators are stdlib-only so that no implementation decides an expected value. The generator implements a declared subset (type, properties, required, oneOf, anyOf, const, local $ref) and refuses a scenario schema that uses anything outside it, and every schema verdict is cross-checked against keripy's validator, a disagreement being investigated rather than adopted. Daniel agreed 2026-10-09 (Q-VQZ0). Rejected depending on a third-party JSON Schema library, which would let that library's bugs become suite law and break the generators' stdlib rule. Tradeoff: a bug in the subset evaluator would become law where the cross-check agrees by coincidence, and schemas using unsupported keywords cannot be tested until the subset grows.
    ACDC levels follow a stated grading calculus = decision:
      nid: 8ky5jvcp
      why: >-
        Every ACDC and exchange-message assertion's level is derived by one calculus, written in design.md. A keyworded clause binds a validator directly only if its subject is a validator, a verifier or a verification procedure, or it states a condition for the message to be valid; a clause that defines a value (a SAID computation, an operator's effective list) binds a validator only through a validator-facing clause that requires checking that value; every other keyworded clause binds producers, and a validator's refusal of its violation is an inferred SHOULD. A refusal's level is the highest, over the derivations that independently refuse, of the lowest level along each derivation, a rule defined for AND and default edge composition only. A link resting on MAY or deferring to an EGF leaves its derivation ungraded in the normative profile, and a link resting on an analogy or only on an example makes it an inferred SHOULD. Where ACDC v1.0 leaves bytes unpinned (the version-string size and JSON form of the most compact serialization, the AGID pre-image, a schema's serialization, the code of a blinded state's ts), a refusal stays MUST only if it holds under every candidate reading, and an acceptance that depends on the choice is SHOULD with inferred_from naming it. Where a keyworded field list contradicts keripy (upd's ta against td), cases follow the text and keripy is disputed. Chosen after the acdc-design keri-review-panel (KRT-F3, SPC-F4, SPC-F1, SPC-F3, SPC-F6, CSR-F1, CSR-F2, CSR-F5, CSR-F7): the design's rule grading a refusal at the highest of its steps' levels was a bare maximum that a literal reader would use to lift a hedged propagation to MUST, and its producer-versus-validator test was unstated, so levels such as that of an expanded-form SAID refusal were set by intuition and could not be reproduced. Rejected leaving levels to each case author's judgment, which two authors apply differently. Tradeoff: fewer ACDC MUSTs, since the selective-disclosure acceptance that depends on the AGID pre-image falls to SHOULD, and the generator must record each derivation rather than only the deciding step. Revised 2026-10-09 after the acdc-exn-cases keri-review-panel (CSR-F2, SEC-F7): the compact form's version-string size is no longer an open reading, because sizing it to the presented form would give each form its own SAID, which line 134 forbids; the presented-size reading survives only as a cross-check diagnostic. A registry head is graded MUST only where a SAID check alone decides it, as when the first update fails its SAID and the head stays at the inception; when the failing update follows counted ones, the head also depends on chain rules that bind the issuer and is SHOULD, inferred.
    Conformance results are published on a static site = decision:
      nid: 5s5wqrcp
      why: >-
        A suite that only runs locally leaves every conformance claim as a private assertion, and the point of the suite is to show publicly which implementations agree and where. Daniel asked (2026-10-09) for each run to produce machine-readable results and for a polished static site, on GitHub Pages and possibly built with Zensical, that carries the README, the user docs and a page of current results. Chose: the runner's existing JSON conformance report is the result format, so nothing new is invented between a run and its publication; results live in the repository under results/, one file per implementation, version and profile, validated against a schema and bounded before the site build reads them, because a submitted report is untrusted input; each result is labelled by provenance, reproduced (produced by this repository's CI from a pinned adapter and implementation) or submitted (sent by a maintainer through a pull request, and shown as their claim), since the suite can vouch only for runs it performed; the results page is generated from those files at build time and shows each result against the suite version it ran, never mixing versions in one comparison. The docs site is built from docs/ and the README by a pinned static site generator and deployed by a workflow on pushes to main. Rejected a hosted service or database, which a static, versioned record does not need and a donated project would have to keep running, and rejected publishing only CI-reproduced results, which would leave out every implementation the suite cannot build. Tradeoff: submitted results are claims the suite cannot verify, so the page must make the distinction impossible to miss; and Zensical is pre-1.0, so it is pinned exactly and kept replaceable (its input is plain markdown). Revised 2026-10-09 while building it: reproduced results are not committed but produced at site-build time by the Pages workflow from the adapter and profile pairs CI already baselines, and results/ holds only submitted results and refuses any file labelled reproduced, so a pull request can never claim that CI ran it; the cost is that reproduced results for older suite versions are not kept. Every result is re-checked before rendering: its verdict and summary must equal what the runner computes from its own case records.
    Common practice is a tested tier of its own = decision:
      nid: 6cp6vv7q
      why: >-
        Much of what deployments depend on has no normative text: the ACDC specification's IPEX section declares itself non-normative while carrying keyword sentences, the IPEX and PTEL drafts are informational, governance frameworks such as the vLEI EGF add rules, and implementations share behaviour no clause states. Triage as written could only call such sentences out of scope, which read as 'does not matter', and behaviour with no sentence was counted nowhere. Daniel chose (2026-10-09, Q-WVVX) a separate practice tier: a triage scope 'practice' for keyword sentences in sections that disclaim normativity, reported in its own coverage column; a practice catalogue for behaviour with no keyword sentence; practice cases whose assertions are level INTEROP with a 'basis' instead of a clause, in non-normative profiles that get an interoperable or not-interoperable verdict shown apart from the conformance verdict and never affecting it. A practice's basis must cite an informational text, a governance framework, or agreement between at least two independent implementations; 'keripy does it' alone is never a basis, because the spec-authority rule exists to stop the suite enshrining keripy as law. The tier starts with IPEX, which SEDI depends on and where the gap between practice and normative text is widest. Rejected leaving practice untested, which hides the part of the protocol most deployments exercise, and rejected folding it into conformance, which would make the suite the specification. Tradeoff: 'common practice' is contestable, so every practice case states its basis and a reader can disagree with it, and the tier invites scope creep, which starting with IPEX alone contains.
    The signify-ts adapter tests a pinned fork's stream parser = decision:
      nid: 2fufssce
      stage-status: in-progress
      why: >-
        Released signify-ts (0.4.0 on npm, 0.4.1 on main) is a KERIA client: it has CESR primitives and event builders but no stream parser, and leaves KEL, ACDC and exchange-message validation to KERIA, so an adapter on a release could honestly answer only cesr.encode. Daniel's fork, dhh1128/signify-ts, adds an exported CESR 1.x and 2.x stream parser in three stacked pull requests to WebOfTrust/signify-ts (#402, #403, #413). Chose an adapter pinned by commit to the top of that stack, rebased onto upstream main, implementing cesr.parse and cesr.encode only, because the parser is signify-ts's own code in signify-ts's own tree and the suite exists to test exactly such a parser before it ships. The adapter reports what parse() returns: a stream is accepted when parse() reports no error, and any reported error is a rejection whose class is the parser's own error code, including incomplete, which this protocol treats as final; nodes the parser marks unknown but frames and continues past are reported as parsed, since second-guessing the parser would make the adapter the thing tested. Item offsets are the parser's spans; raw values and indexes come from signify-ts's Matter and Indexer applied to the bytes at those spans; a genus code's version is rendered from the count the parser read. The adapter starts Node with --disable-wasm-trap-handler, because libsodium's WebAssembly cannot reserve memory under the runner's 4 GiB address-space limit otherwise. Rejected waiting for the pull requests to merge, which leaves the parser untested while it is under review, and rejected an adapter that frames streams itself on top of released primitives, which the integrity rule forbids. Tradeoff: the implementation under test is an unreleased fork written by the suite's own author, so the adapter names the fork and commit in hello and its README says so; it must be re-pinned to a signify-ts release once the parser lands, and results against the fork say nothing about released signify-ts.
    The keripy adapter is checked weekly against other keripy refs = decision:
      nid: 4numcxwq
      stage-status: in-progress
      why: >-
        CI pins the keripy adapter to one keripy commit per generation, but keripy is used at many refs: upstream main moves weekly, Bakobo and Provenant run forks, and Provenant deploys pinned 1.2.x and 2.0.0-dev builds. A survey on 2026-10-09 ran the unchanged adapter against six such refs and every one reproduced its generation's baselines except for the commit it reported, which is the property worth guarding: the day a ref diverges is the day someone should look. Chose a weekly workflow, plus a manual trigger, that reads a committed list of refs (adapters/keripy/refs.json), resolves each to a commit with git ls-remote, and installs the adapter against that commit only when the commit, the ref's own entry, or the suite's own inputs (cases, profiles, runner, keripy adapter) changed since the last completed run; the last run's state lives in the Actions cache, so a quiet week costs one short job. A ref that was run is compared with the committed baseline of each of its profiles, ignoring only the implementation's commit, which must instead be exactly the commit the ref resolved to, and any other difference fails the job; a ref skipped because nothing changed still fails if its last run differed, so a divergence stays red without being re-run. Rejected nightly runs and per-ref baselines, which spend runner minutes and review effort on refs that rarely move, and rejected a job per ref, which GitHub bills a minimum minute each even when it only decides to skip. Tradeoff: a cache eviction costs one full run, a ref whose divergence is accepted needs its entry removed or a baseline of its own, and Provenant's 1.1.x refs, which the adapter cannot load, are left out.
    Live multi-wallet interop runs sit beside the cases, never inside them = decision:
      nid: 64nrddzd
      why: >-
        Opens why-acdc G17 (heti tick 6bec): show that heti and Veridian hold and present the same ACDC to one verifier, and that a holder moves between them without reissue. That evidence is a scripted end-to-end run against live services (witnesses, a KERIA agent, Veridian's own src/core driven headlessly in Node, and heti in Python), not a fixed case with a normative verdict, so it cannot live in cases/ without breaking 'No case without a normative clause' and 'Fixtures are generated'. It goes under interop/<run>/, here interop/two-wallets/, with its own README stating that a pass is an observation about two pinned implementations on one day, not a conformance claim. Chose this repo over heti or a new repo because the suite already holds the signify-ts and keripy adapters and is where cross-implementation evidence is meant to accumulate (Daniel, 2026-10-12); keeping it out of heti stops Node, Docker and KERIA leaking into heti's test surface. Each run pins every implementation by commit or image digest and records the incompatibilities it found as its primary output, because the incompatibilities, not the green run, are what a reader acts on. Not CI-gating at first: a live run needs Docker and builds KERIA from source, and a flaky gate would teach people to ignore it; it is runnable on demand and its last result is committed. Falsifier: if live runs start being cited as conformance, they have drifted into the case corpus's role and the line must be redrawn. The repo is public, so nothing embargoed may appear in a run's writeup.
    SEDI can claim interoperability under a versioned deployment profile = goal:
      nid: 2u40l9tk
      why: >-
        Utah's KERI-001 asks for a 'Versioned support profile for KERI, CESR, ACDC, registry events, seals, and IPEX' with 'Canonical test vectors agreed across issuer, holder, and verifier'. Daniel decided 2026-10-14 (profile-design interview, Q1) that this is SEDI's deployment profile, named sedi-1: a non-normative profile in this suite whose basis is the SEDI profile version, layered over cesr-v1.0, keri-v1.0.1 and acdc-v1.0 as prerequisites, edited by Bakobo until a SEDI body (Utah's Interop WG) owns it, versioned independently of the suite and pinning the suite version and each spec commit, with every version ratified by Daniel. Rejected a neutral community profile, whose choices would each need two independent implementations as a basis, which SEDI lacks while heti and KERIA both rest on keripy. Tradeoff: the suite handed to the KERI Foundation carries one state's deployment profile, which the profile mechanism must keep visibly separate from conformance.
      children:
        The SEDI profile picks where the spec is silent, and says so = decision:
          nid: 8g9vtxvc
          why: >-
            Daniel agreed 2026-10-14 (interview Q2). sedi-1 settles every ambiguity SEDI needs in order to interoperate, as a numbered profile choice carrying the reading chosen, why, the keri-sow tick that tracks it, and the spec text that would overturn it; each choice is also drafted as a spec question for Daniel to raise. The first set: TEL n is prior plus one (57ud); current registry state is the highest-n event that chain-verifies and is anchored, and two anchored events at one n are refused (5qcy); keripy's Blinder derivation (3b5o, flagged as contested upstream); ACDC #200's bulk defaults (4xwz); -S## and the 8-character group code, a seal is the couple {s,d} only, edge d is required (3nch); an unknown operator is not evaluable (3qah); absent o defaults per v1.1 (7kev); o may be scalar or list (6ljr); a verifier checks presenter equals a.i (5u4y). A spec answer that differs produces a new profile version, never an edit. The normative profiles never pick, and keep disputed and inferred-SHOULD. Rejected refusing to cover open areas, which would leave the profile silent on registries, bulk issuance and operators, exactly what Utah asked for. Tradeoff: SEDI may pick a reading the working group later rejects, and its implementations must then migrate.
        SEDI targets the 2.00 wire; 1.x is a migration companion = decision:
          nid: 43rj9wfc
          why: >-
            Daniel agreed 2026-10-14 (interview Q3). sedi-1 tests KERI 2.00, CESR genus 2.00 and ACDC 2.00, JSON only, Ed25519 and Blake3-256 only, the SEDI schemas, rip with both upd and bup registries, operators I2I, NI2I, DI2I, E1E, AND and OR, and chain-strict propagation; NOT waits for 67i2, Merkle batch seals wait for proof semantics (KERI-002/003), and bulk issuance enters as draft citing a named ACDC main commit because #204 and #207 are open. Chose 2.00 because Sam's SEDI vectors, the published SEDI schemas and acdc-v1.0 all use it. The deployed 1.x stack (KERIA, signify-ts, Veridian, as G17 ran it in interop/two-wallets) gets a companion profile sedi-1x-interop, for holder migration and not as a target. Rejected a general ACDC profile with SEDI as an extension, since acdc-v1.0 already is that. Tradeoff: if Utah answers that it deploys 1.x, the companion leads in practice while the target stays 2.00.
        SEDI vectors are one corpus read three ways = decision:
          nid: 63v08x06
          why: >-
            Daniel agreed 2026-10-14 (interview Q4). 'Agreed across issuer, holder, and verifier' means a producer must match bytes and a consumer must match verdicts over the same corpus. Issuers are tested by a new adapter operation acdc.issue, as keri.emit tests KERI producers: seeds, salts, stamps and attributes in, ACDC plus registry and anchor events out, bodies checked byte for byte and signatures verified. Holders are tested by acdc.present: a bundle and a disclosure choice in, a presentation out, checked by bytes where disclosure is deterministic and otherwise by acdc.verify agreeing with the full form; a holder must also refuse what a verifier refuses. Verifiers use acdc.verify. Sam's test_sedi.py literals are cross-checks for the suite's own spec-driven generator, never expected values, and his KELs cannot be validation input because they pass raw next keys as ndigs. A heti adapter is built inline with the profile work (Daniel: 'build it soon... ideally inline'), so results come from keripy, heti and, on the 1x companion, signify-ts. Rejected live multi-party runs as vectors; those stay under interop/. Tradeoff: two new operations to specify, schema-check and probe.
        SEDI grades unresolved apart from invalid = decision:
          nid: 8fh68wt6
          why: >-
            Daniel agreed 2026-10-14 (interview Q5). Utah's KERI-012 asks to 'test positive, negative, and unresolved verification outcomes', which is a relying party's choice between rejecting and retrying. acdc-v1.0 leaves invalid versus incomplete informative; sedi-1 grades it at INTEROP: invalid when a check failed, incomplete when something the verdict needs is absent (KEL, registry event, far node, schema) or cannot be evaluated (unknown operator, unsupported registry kind, undecidable edge), and never valid in either. Revoked reads as not valid as a SEDI profile choice, since the spec has no revoked verdict. An unreachable KEL is modelled as one absent from the bundle, so verdict cases stay offline and fixed. Every refusal keeps a positive pair. Rejected leaving the distinction to reports, which does not test unresolved outcomes. Tradeoff: an implementation that reports every failure as invalid passes acdc-v1.0 and fails sedi-1.
        A holder-assembled bundle never proves more than it carries = decision:
          nid: 3g4le6js
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-14 (Q-RZGS), from a security review of the sedi-1 test plan. The bundle is the whole world and the holder assembles it, so three profile choices close the ways a holder or issuer can make withheld or weaker evidence read as good. First, registry evidence the verdict did not account for makes it never valid: a later event after a gap, a later event that fails its chain link, a registry event the issuer's KEL anchors but the bundle does not carry, or an anchored blinded update whose state is undisclosed gives incomplete when evidence is absent and invalid when a check fails, never valid at the earlier head; without this, dropping event n=2 leaves a revoked credential's head at issued (57ud and 5qcy as first written). Second, under sedi-1 a 1.x version string, a 1.x field set or a 1.x registry is never valid, so a credential revoked in its 2.00 registry cannot be re-presented in its 1.x form; sedi-1x-interop grades 1.x on its own and never prints a sedi-1 claim. Third, when a bundle holds both a failed check and something not evaluable, the verdict is invalid, so adding an unknown operator to a forged credential cannot turn a reject into a retry. docs/profiles/sedi.md also states that incomplete is never an accept, provisional or otherwise, and that a valid verdict holds as of the registry head the report prints. Rejected leaving these to relying-party policy, which is the gap an attacker uses. Tradeoff: an honest holder with a partial registry gets incomplete and must fetch more, rather than a valid it could have had.
        A presentation carries who presented it, to whom = decision:
          nid: 09snlyre
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-14 (Q-RZGS). Choice 5u4y (the presenter must be the issuee a.i) had no operation that could observe it: acdc.verify carries no presenter and exn.verify does not judge the ACDC it names. Chose an optional presentation context on acdc.verify (the grant exn stream and the verifier's AID), added to the message schema and probed by kcs check-adapter, so presenter binding and replay to a second verifier become fixed offline cases; absent the context, acdc.verify behaves as today. For the aggregate credential (keri-sow 5oo6) sedi-1 reads the issuee at A[1].i, as keripy's iseaid does; an aggregate whose issuee is undisclosed cannot be bound and is incomplete when a context is given, and an untargeted credential is a bearer credential, which docs/profiles/sedi.md says plainly so Utah sees it as a policy fact rather than inheriting it. Rejected a composite case routed through exn.verify, which splits one verdict across two operations. Tradeoff: acdc.verify's request grows an optional field every adapter must parse or reject cleanly.
        A case's grant names the presented ACDC and nothing more = decision:
          nid: 7krs5hfz
          why: >-
            The presentation context (09snlyre) ends with the message that carries or names the presented ACDC, but no normative text fixes a grant's shape: KERI v1.0.1 gives only the exn field set (line 1197), and the ACDC specification's IPEX section is non-normative (line 1797). So sedi-1's cases send one exn with route /ipex/grant, ri the verifier's AID, x and p empty because it begins no transaction (KERI 979), q empty, and a = {o: [the presented ACDC's SAID]}, the origin list keripy 2.x's ipexing.grant writes. The ACDC travels as the request's presented, with its registry and KELs in the bundle, so the grant names it rather than nesting a second copy. The sedi-1 model reads the context in order: the last message must be accepted by the suite's exchange-message model (exn_build), else the presentation proves no presenter and the verdict is invalid; its a.o must be exactly the presented ACDC's SAID, else invalid (presentation-names); its ri must be the verifier, else invalid (presentation-verifier, the replay to a second verifier); then presenter-binding (5u4y) applies to its sender i. Rejected keripy 2.x's grant whole: its x is a random digest with no xip before it, which KERI 979 refuses, and its nested artifacts and heti's a.tel and a.far are one implementation's evidence channel, not a shape the suite may enshrine (9vnp6fz7). Tradeoff: an implementation that admits only its own grant shape (heti, keripy 2.x) cannot take the suite's grant as is, so its adapter must judge the context with the implementation's parts, and what the case then tests is that composition, which the adapter's README must say.
        The suite grades a holder, never the holder's own verifier = decision:
          nid: 3lq4zrr3
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-14 (Q-RZGS), revising 63v08x06. A presentation whose bytes are not deterministic was to be checked by acdc.verify agreeing with the full form; if that verdict came from the same adapter, the adapter grades itself and an always-invalid verifier makes any output agree. Chose the suite's own decision-procedure model as the oracle, applied by the runner to the returned bytes, plus two checks the verdict cannot express: the presentation carries the full form's top-level SAID and its disclosed blocks recompute to the SAIDs the case expects, and no secret the disclosure hides (attribute values, their salts, undisclosed blinded state, a hidden issuee block) appears in the response in any encoding the suite enumerates. A holder refuses with a fixed result shape, so refusal is scoreable rather than an error that fails every assertion. Rejected trusting the adapter's verifier. Tradeoff: the runner carries the ACDC model, and its own bugs become grading bugs, which the model's tests must bear; and because the runner depends on nothing at runtime (4fmwzdjs) while the model needs Blake3 and Ed25519, which the standard library lacks, the runner vendors pure-Python implementations of both, bounded by input size and checked against the generator's pinned libraries, and until they land a non-deterministic holder case is incomplete rather than passing.
        A presentation is graded by its body's bytes, its attachments by their shape = decision:
          nid: 37ehqmh4
          why: >-
            Refines 3lq4zrr3 for the first holder cases. A presentation's body is fixed by the held ACDC and the disclosure asked for, in every form the protocol defines (compact, expanded, partial, selective), so the runner compares the framed body with the case's expected bytes exactly; that also enforces the binding rule, since the top-level SAID and every disclosed block's SAID are in the body. Its attachments are not fixed: a holder may point to its issuer's commitment with a seal source couple or carry nothing, because under sedi-1 a verifier finds the commitment through the registry. So the runner checks only that what follows the body is empty or one attachments group of seal source couples at -S (each a 0A sequence number and an E digest), since a presentation has nothing else to attach, and that the stream is within the runner's stream bound. Leaks are checked apart from bytes: the case lists each secret the disclosure hides, and the runner fails it if any appears in the response raw, as hex, Base64, Base64url or JSON-escaped. A refusal is graded by its shape. Rejected grading attachments by bytes, which would fail a holder for choosing a pointer the profile allows, and grading the whole presentation through the suite's verifier model at the runner, which would carry the generator's sedi model into the runtime (4fmwzdjs) for no gain while the body is deterministic. Tradeoff: a holder whose attachments are well formed but wrong (a couple naming another event) passes the bytes check; the verifier cases are what grade such a pointer.
        sedi-1 bounds what a credential may hold = decision:
          nid: 7qx6tl5p
          why: >-
            Test-plan item 13 asks sedi-1 to state maxima an implementation must hold to, with cases at each maximum and just above it. Chose: 64 events per registry (the runner's own bound on a returned registry, issued.MAX_EVENTS, so a sedi-1 issuer never returns more than a sedi-1 verifier reads), 32 attribute blocks per aggregate, edge groups nested 8 deep (the depth the provenance DAG is already bounded at), 8 operators in one o list, 4096 bytes in one attribute value, measured as a whole (a string's UTF-8, anything else's compact JSON), and 44 characters in any u, the longest primitive a u holds (a 256-bit blinding factor, code a; a salt is 24), measured by its size whatever its type. Each is large enough for every SEDI credential the plan names and small enough that a verifier holding to it is bounded in work. A credential past a maximum is not a sedi-1 credential: the reference model refuses it as invalid, and its case expects not-valid, so an implementation that refuses to evaluate it (incomplete) also conforms, and the case is marked beyond-bound, the one exemption from a sedi-1 case naming the model's exact verdict. Every maximum is checked on the whole credential before it is evaluated, never inside the edge evaluation, so an OR group's unneeded alternative cannot hide a breach. Rejected bounding only at the runner, which leaves an implementation free to hang on an input the profile could have ruled out. Tradeoff: a SEDI credential that legitimately needs more than a maximum needs a new profile version.
        Issuer and holder operations take templates and return bytes = decision:
          nid: 4xw8k0hg
          why: >-
            Shapes the operations 63v08x06, 09snlyre and 3lq4zrr3 call for, in protocol version 1 while it is still a draft. acdc.issue takes the issuer's KEL as keri.process would deliver it, seeds for the issuer's current keys, and templates: the ACDC, each registry event, and the anchoring key event, each a JSON object with every field given and every SAID field (and v) left as the empty string, which is how keripy's own builders take an issuance (Compactor over a bare map) and how heti's do. A blindable update's blinded state is given as a template plus the 128-bit shared salt it is derived from, so the implementation's derivation (3b5o) is what is tested. The anchoring event's seals are literal, because a generated case already knows every SAID, and a placeholder language for them would be adapter computation under another name; so the seal shape is tested on the verifier side (3nch), not here. The result is the ACDC, each registry event with its blinded state block, and the signed anchoring event, all as hex bytes the runner compares exactly, or a refusal {refused: {reason}}, which is also acdc.present's refusal, so declining to issue or present is scoreable rather than an error. acdc.present takes acdc.verify's bundle with the held ACDC fully expanded and a disclosure choice (compact, expanded, partial with the block paths to expand, or selective with the aggregate labels to disclose) and returns the presented ACDC with its attachments, as acdc.verify's presented takes it. The presentation context is an optional acdc.verify field holding the exchange messages that delivered the ACDC and the verifier's AID; because an adapter ignores fields it does not recognise, a context case is sent only to an adapter declaring the feature acdc.presentation, which sedi-1 requires, so ignoring the context cannot pass a sedi-1 claim. The two new operations need no feature: the runner already sends a case only to an adapter listing its operation. Rejected a high-level issuance request (schema, attributes, recipient), which every implementation would map to its own defaults, so the bytes would test the adapter's mapping. Tradeoff: an implementation whose issuance API cannot take a given salt or stamp needs its adapter to fix them, as the clock rule already requires.
        A layered profile's claim is computed over its prerequisites in the same run = decision:
          nid: 47yreej2
          why: >-
            Settles how the runner makes the claim 2u40l9tk describes ('conforms to cesr-v1.0, keri-v1.0.1 and acdc-v1.0, and is interoperable under sedi-1'). A profile file may name prerequisites, a version, pins (suite version and each spec commit, by full hash), required operations and features, choices and maxima; such a file is a layered profile. kcs run --profile reads the named profile file and refuses a name with no file (a distinct error code, never an empty run that reads as no-evidence), and for a layered profile runs its own cases and every prerequisite's in one session, so a prerequisite passed in an earlier run never counts. The claim takes the definite failures first, as 3g4le6js orders a single verdict: not-interoperable if a prerequisite is not-conformant, a required operation or feature is not declared, or the profile's own INTEROP verdict is not-interoperable; otherwise incomplete if a prerequisite or the profile's own verdict is incomplete or no-evidence; otherwise the profile's own verdict. The report carries the claim beside the per-profile verdicts and never words an interoperability verdict as conformance. A choice names the cases that exercise it, at least one positive and one negative; while the profile version is draft an unfilled choice is listed by the generator, and the generator refuses to mark a version active with one, so the profile can land in steps without hiding the gap. A released version's choices, prerequisites, pins, requirements and maxima are frozen by a recorded hash, and changing any of them needs a new version; its case list and prose may grow. Rejected letting a prerequisite's verdict come from a separate report, which cannot show the adapter, commit and suite were the same, and rejected a new verdict value for a refused claim, which would add exit codes for what the existing four already say. Tradeoff: while acdc-v1.0's cases are draft its verdict is no-evidence, so no sedi-1 claim can be interoperable until an implementation has evaluated them and they go active.
        sedi-1 requires the blinded registry only = decision:
          nid: 8dgunr0e
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-15 (Q-FEYE), revising 43rj9wfc, which had sedi-1 test rip under both upd and bup registries. heti has retired the public upd registry and issues only blinded bup updates, and Sam Smith's SEDI tests in keripy use bup, so requiring upd would fail heti by design over a registry model no SEDI implementation deploys. sedi-1 requires acdc.registry.bup and not acdc.registry.upd; its upd cases are sent only to an adapter declaring acdc.registry.upd, and a mixed upd and bup head stays a holder-side case for such adapters. Rejected keeping upd required and recording heti's gap as a divergence, which would make every heti claim not-interoperable for a choice SEDI never made. Tradeoff: an implementation that issues only upd registries can no longer claim sedi-1, and a later version that wants upd back needs a profile version bump.
        sedi-1 accepts the event seal {i, s, d} as well as the couple {s, d} = decision:
          nid: 2082s6k4
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-15 (Q-QYG7), revising choice 3nch's seal-shape reading under 8g9vtxvc. ACDC line 1922 shows a registry or issuance seal as the couple {s, d}, but heti and keripy's SEDI tests both anchor with the event seal {i, s, d}, so the couple-only reading fails both SEDI implementations on every issuance. sedi-1 accepts {s, d}, and {i, s, d} whose i is the registry's SAID, as a KERI event seal names the log its event belongs to; an i naming anything else, an extra field and a bare digest are refused. Corrected the same day: the question put to Daniel said i is the issuer, but heti writes i as the registry's SAID (heti registries.py _tel_seal), and accepting the seal the SEDI implementations actually write is what the approval was for. This widens line 1922, and docs/profiles/sedi.md says so in plain words; the question goes to Sam as a draft in utah-keri-adoption/plan/drafts/, never posted by a session. Rejected the couple-only reading, which grades both deployed implementations as failing for a shape the spec's own event seals use. Tradeoff: a verifier that implements line 1922 literally refuses SEDI's seals, and sedi-1 grades that verifier as failing the choice.
        sedi-1 grades operator shapes the text leaves open, and follows it where it speaks = decision:
          nid: 599d5f5f
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-15 (Q-M8W7), revising the operator-shape cases of the sedi-1 test plan, which graded o: [] and [I2I, NI2I] invalid. ACDC v1.0 line 1197 says an empty o gets the default operator appended, and line 1186 says the latest of conflicting unary operators takes precedence, so o: [] reads as an absent o and [I2I, NI2I] reads as NI2I; v1.1 keeps both rules (lines 1197 and 1209). Where the text says nothing, sedi-1 records a choice, operator-shape: an empty AND or OR edge-group and a nested list such as [[I2I]] are invalid, because each is a malformed instruction rather than one the verifier cannot evaluate, while a list member that names no known operator ([I2I, XYZ], [1]) or an o that is an object is incomplete under unknown-operator. Rejected grading the spec-covered shapes invalid, which would fail an implementation for following the text. Tradeoff: an issuer can write [I2I, NI2I] and get NI2I, so a contradictory list relaxes rather than fails, which the profile prose names.
        sedi-1x-interop is built by the suite and judged as keripy 1.2.14 judges it = decision:
          nid: 9sgwng6m
          why: >-
            Test-plan item 11 turns G17's incompatibilities 7, 8, 10 and 11 (interop/two-wallets/RESULT.md) into cases with positive twins, at INTEROP, plus a cross-version revocation case. A 1.x credential is not decided by any normative text (docs/design.md, ACDC), so sedi-1x-interop is non-normative, like keripy-1x-interop, and its basis is keripy 1.2.14: its expected verdicts are what keripy 1.2.14's Parser, Tevery and Verifier conclude on each bundle. Chose to build every 1.x bundle with the suite's own builder, as the CESR interop cases are, and cross-check it against keripy 1.2.14 in its pinned environment (generators/keripy1x_check), so the vectors never depend on keripy to exist and a disagreement is recorded rather than absorbed: a 1.x KEL (KERI10JSON version strings, 1.00 count codes, controller signatures at -A), a vcp/iss/rev registry whose events carry the anchoring ixn as seal source couples at -G, and a 1.x ACDC (ri for rd, no t, its SAID over the expanded form) carrying its iss event as a seal source triple at -I. Each case is an acdc.verify case that goes only to an adapter declaring acdc.keripy-1x and acdc.ptel-1x, which keripy 1.2.14's adapter will; the bundle needs no presentation context, since keripy 1.x judges an ACDC without one. A sedi-1x-interop result never enters a sedi-1 claim, and a run of both reports them apart. Rejected generating the vectors by driving keripy 1.2.14's own issuer, which makes keripy both the source and the judge, so the cross-check could find nothing. Rejected running the companion against signify-ts now: it has no local ACDC verifier (it leaves validation to KERIA). Daniel decided 2026-10-20 (Q-V1FN) to build the companion against keripy 1.2.14 only, with incompatibility 8 recorded as a verifier case on the a.dt form, and to move a KERIA-backed signify-ts adapter to its own plan item, signify-keria-adapter. Tradeoff: a second, small issuance builder for a wire nothing normative defines, kept honest only by the keripy cross-check.
        A cross-role agreement matrix verifies what each adapter issues and presents with every adapter = decision:
          nid: 3ptj8hu9
          why: >-
            Test-plan item 10 asks the evidence pack to verify each adapter's acdc.issue output, its KELs included, with every adapter's acdc.verify, and each presentation the same way, deterministic, offline and reported per cell, and not as a graded assertion. Chose a runner subcommand, kcs agree, that takes two or more named adapters and the suite's acdc.issue and acdc.present cases. For each case and each producer it sends the case as kcs run would and grades the answer with the case's own assertions; then it turns the answer into acdc.verify bundles with nothing added: every returned KEL split one event per stream, each credential's registry split one event per genus-pinned stream with the head update carrying its disclosure as a -C group around a -a group, the request's schemas, the far credentials the presented one's edges reach, and the ACDC as presented. A presentation keeps its case's bundle and replaces held with the holder's presented stream. Every verifier that declares acdc.verify and the case's features judges every bundle, the producer included, and each cell records case, producer, verifier, credential, what was produced, the verdict and whether it agrees. A cell expects valid when the case expects an issuance or presentation and the producer's answer passes every assertion of the case, and expects not valid when the case expects an issuer's refusal and the issuer issued anyway, so an issuer negative case must be one whose fault a verifier can see in the bytes, as a schema breach or an edge that cannot hold is. Otherwise the cell has no expectation and is recorded only: a refusal produces no bundle, an answer that fails its own case is the producer's failure and is already graded by kcs run, and a holder that presents what it must refuse may have presented something valid in another form. The tool exits 1 when any cell disagrees, so a pack built in CI notices, and never feeds a profile verdict. Rejected comparing the verifiers' verdicts with each other rather than with an expectation, which counts two verifiers that both accept a broken issuance as agreeing; rejected using the reference model as a verifier, which is generator-side and would put generator code in the runner. Tradeoff: with one adapter the matrix holds only self-agreement, which shows the adapter's issuer and verifier agree and nothing about another implementation.
        An issuer is graded by verifying what it issued, never by its bytes = decision:
          nid: 2u2ps8mt
          why: >-
            Daniel decided 2026-10-15 (Q-NKZ9), revising the issuer half of 4xw8k0hg, which had acdc.issue take seeds and templates with every salt, stamp and seal literal and compare the result byte for byte. heti mints its own keys, draws its registry nonces and blinding salts at random and stamps the wall clock, which is careful, correct issuance, and a suite that cannot grade it is the broken party. So acdc.issue asks for an issuance, not for bytes: the request names the identifiers the implementation is to create by role, and the credentials to issue among them, each with its schema (raw), its attribute values, its issuee (a role, or none), its edges (to other requested credentials, with their operators) and its registry kind; the result returns each role's KEL, and each credential's ACDC with its registry events and blinded state disclosures. The runner grades the result by verifying it: every KEL event's signatures against its own key state, every SAID and BLID recomputed, every registry event and the ACDC anchored in the issuer's KEL in a seal shape the profile accepts, and the profile's required fields and shapes (field set and order, registry kind, operator form). It then compares each issued ACDC with what was asked after normalizing the fields that legitimately vary (SAIDs, salts u, registry nonces, stamps dt, identifier prefixes and keys), and compares bytes only where the request fully determines them. A blinding derivation (3b5o) is checked when the result also returns the shared salt, and is otherwise reported incomplete, never passed. Negative issuer cases become requests an implementation must refuse (an attribute its schema forbids, an edge to a credential whose issuee is not the new issuer under I2I, a forbidden field), and output that fails verification fails the case whatever was asked. The runner's verification is the same decision-procedure model 3lq4zrr3 has it carry for holders, so issuer and holder grading share one model and its tests. kcs check-adapter drops the issue-determinism probe and instead checks that two identical requests each yield output that verifies. Rejected keeping templates and byte comparison with seeds and stamps the adapter must force, which grades an implementation's test hooks rather than its issuance, and 93t4d880 forbids patching heti to add them. Tradeoff: an issuer bug that yields a different but still verifiable credential (another salt length within bounds, say) passes unless a shape check names it, and the runner carries enough of the model to verify KELs and registries as well as ACDCs.
        A credential whose schema forbids rd is issued directly, by a digest seal of its SAID = decision:
          nid: 947dh20t
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-20 (Q-BZB9, option a). sedi-1 commits a credential only through its registry (8g9vtxvc), but the SEDI IAR schema in keripy's SEDI tests (tests/sedi/test_sedi.py at 4c0a6fe, EFAB6k77bXHs6bg9PORW7UYF79GD_OuEcEjmBpwhcfRN) has no rd and closes its top level, so an IAR can never name a registry and every IAR would be invalid under sedi-1. ACDC v1.0 line 1667 gives the other case: with no registry, the issuer anchors an issuance proof digest seal in its KEL whose value is the ACDC's own SAID, and the ACDC has one state, issued. sedi-1 records a choice, direct-issuance: a credential without rd whose schema forbids one (its top level lists no rd and closes with additionalProperties false; a closing subschema may still admit some registry SAID, which no finite check can rule out) is committed by the digest seal {d} naming its SAID in the issuer's KEL trunk, and cannot be revoked; a seal naming its SAID in any other shape is refused (invalid), and no seal leaves it incomplete, as a missing registry anchor does. A credential without rd whose schema would admit one, whether it lists rd or leaves its top level open, is still uncommitted (invalid), so an issuer cannot put a revocable credential beyond revocation by leaving out its rd; and while the schema itself is withheld the verifier cannot tell which rule applies, so that credential is incomplete rather than invalid. An identity assurance receipt reads as a one-time attestation, which is what its schema implies. Rejected leaving IAR outside sedi-1, which drops a credential SEDI issues, and asking Sam to add rd to the IAR schema, which changes a schema already pinned by SAID in his tests; whether an IAR should be revocable goes to Utah in Daniel's batch. Rejected reading a schema that merely does not list rd as registry-less: the suite's own verifier schemas leave their top level open without listing rd, and so would most schemas written before SEDI, so such a reading would quietly take every credential that drops its rd out of revocation. Tradeoff: a directly issued credential stays valid until its issuer's KEL is compromised, and a closed schema that omits rd by mistake makes its credentials unrevocable under sedi-1.
        sedi-1's blind-derivation and bulk-defaults choices wait for their text, and are never checked in the runner = decision:
          nid: 2c8nsmr7
          why: >-
            Daniel approved 2026-10-20 (Q-EK25). Neither issuer-side choice has a case yet. Checking a blinded state's u under blind-derivation (8g9vtxvc, tick 3b5o) means running Argon2id at 64 MiB, which the runner, standard library only by design (3lq4zrr3), cannot do in reasonable time, and acdc.issue would have to return the shared salt for it to be checked at all (2u2ps8mt reports it incomplete otherwise); its reading also waits on the spec question drafted for Sam. bulk-defaults rests on ACDC main text (pull request 200) that no tagged release carries. So both are deferred: their cases are written once 3b5o is answered and the bulk text is tagged, and the derivation is then checked in the generator and in an optional evidence tool, never in the runner. Until then sedi-1 stays draft, and the generator lists the four unfilled sides on every run, as 47yreej2 has it do for any unfilled choice. Rejected adding Argon2id to the runner, which breaks its standard-library rule for one check, and rejected writing the cases now against text that may change. Tradeoff: no sedi-1 claim can be made active while the two choices are unfilled, and an issuer's blinding derivation goes ungraded until then.
        acdc.issue asks for a direct issuance by registry kind none = decision:
          nid: 92zkyy59
          why: >-
            Carries direct-issuance (947dh20t) to the issuer side, as 2u2ps8mt grades issuers. A credential entry's registry kind was only bup; it may now be none, which asks the implementation to issue the credential with no registry: the ACDC carries no rd, the result's entry for it carries no registry and no disclosure, and the runner requires the digest seal {d} of the ACDC's SAID on the issuer's verified KEL in place of the registry checks. Which way a case goes is decided by the generator from the schema, as sedi_issue already decides from the attributes and edges: under sedi-1 a request for none under a schema that admits rd must be refused, because the credential would be uncommitted, and a request for bup under a schema that forbids rd must be refused, because the ACDC could not name its registry and still satisfy its schema. The runner does not repeat that decision; it verifies what comes back against the kind asked for. Rejected letting the runner infer the kind from the schema, which would make a request's meaning depend on a rule of one profile, and rejected a separate operation for direct issuance, which would duplicate every other check acdc.issue makes. Tradeoff: an adapter that ignores the registry kind issues a bup registry for a none request and fails the case, which is the honest result for an implementation that cannot issue directly.
    A normative profile is named for its specification text's exact tag = decision:
      nid: 67nf4ghs
      why: >-
        The three normative profiles were named cesr-1.0, keri-1.0 and acdc-1.0, after the specification version. Readers took cesr-1.0 for a version of the case bundle, as if a semver of the suite's CESR cases, and keri-1.0 does not even name the text it cites, which is KERI tag v1.0.1. Daniel decided 2026-10-15 to rename each to the exact tag of the specification text its assertions cite, as pinned in generators/spec_tables/spec_source.py and stated in the profile's spec field: cesr-v1.0, keri-v1.0.1, acdc-v1.0. Non-normative profiles (cesr-strict, keri-escrow, keripy-1x-interop) keep names that say what they are. A normative profile's case list grows from one suite version to the next, so the profile name identifies the cited text and not a frozen set of cases; the frozen claim is profile plus suite version, as the conformance-claim tuple in docs/design.md already states. When a new tag of a specification is pinned, it gets a new profile named for that tag rather than silently changing what an existing name cites. Rejected keeping the 1.0 names with a warning in design.md, which is what acdc-1.0 already did and did not stop the misreading, and rejected a scheme like cesr-spec-1.0, which still drops the patch component KERI needs. Tradeoff: every case file, profile, adapter baseline and doc that names a profile changes once, and a tag with a patch component makes keri-v1.0.1 look unlike its siblings.
      tensions:
      - with: Published expectations are immutable@9ppgdqn5
        nature: >-
          Every case file carries its profile name, so the rename rewrites the profile field of every CESR, KERI and ACDC case already generated. That decision makes a case id and its assertions permanent and design.md says a published case's assertions never change; neither names the profile field, but a reader could take a published case file to be frozen byte for byte, and a conformance claim names its profile.
        resolution: >-
          Taken at suite version 0.0.1, while the suite is incubating and nothing has been released, so no published claim or case exists to change meaning. No case id, assertion, expected value or SAID changes; only the profile field does, and the regeneration was checked to show nothing else moved. After a first release a profile is not renamed in place: a new name means a new profile, with the old one kept for the releases that cite it.
    The heti adapter presents through heti's disclose and judges a presentation with heti's parts = decision:
      nid: 9fg9ufsm
      why: >-
        Test-plan item 12 asks the heti adapter for acdc.present and for acdc.verify's presentation context (feature acdc.presentation), which sedi-1 requires. heti's public API covers part of each. acdc.present: the held ACDC is first judged by heti's own verifier through the adapter's acdc.verify path, and the adapter refuses whatever heti refuses, since a holder refuses what a verifier would. The form then goes to heti's public disclose (heti.anchored.produce.disclosing): compact and partial conceal the top-level blocks the form hides, and selective reveals the aggregate's blocks by index, each label mapped to its block's index. heti can conceal only the attribute section and its sub-blocks, so a form that would compact e or r is refused with that reason rather than compacted by the adapter, and heti always reveals the aggregate's issuee block, so a form that would hide it (compact, or selective without the issuee) is refused rather than answered with a presentation other than the one asked for, which the protocol forbids and which would leak the issuee's salt. The expanded form is the held body unchanged, the one place the adapter answers without a heti call, because the held ACDC is already fully expanded. A presentation carries no attachments, which the protocol allows. Presentation context: heti judges a grant only inside admit, which needs the verifier's own keys, and its grant is its own dialect, so, as 7krs5hfz foresaw, the adapter judges the context with heti's parts. After heti verifies the ACDC, the adapter reads the last message: it must be an exn on /ipex/grant whose a.o names exactly the presented SAID and whose ri is the verifier, else invalid. The grant's signatures are checked by heti's public consume.verify against the sender's key state replayed from the bundle's KELs, at the establishment event the signature group names. The presenter rule is heti's own, from admit (ipex.py): the sender must be the issuee keripy's iseaid reads, or the issuer, so heti's divergence from sedi-1's issuee-only rule (heti tick 6kvm) shows in the results instead of being hidden. The adapter declares acdc.presentation in composes, which needs the feature marked composable in profiles/features.json; it had been false only by default when the feature was added, and 7krs5hfz already says such a case tests the composition. The README says what is composed. Rejected leaving acdc.presentation undeclared, which keeps every verdict heti's but means heti can never claim sedi-1, and rejected composing sedi-1's issuee-only rule, which would credit heti with a check it does not make. Tradeoff: the presentation cases test heti's verifier and signature check plus the adapter's reading of the grant, not heti's admit, until heti offers a public way to judge a grant for a named verifier. Revised 2026-10-20: heti (127d75c, bakobo/heti#63) now exposes its presenter rule as heti.anchored.produce.ipex.bound_issuee, which refuses a credential whose attribute section, aggregate or aggregate issuee block is concealed rather than reading it as an affidavit, so the adapter calls it instead of copying keripy's iseaid, and grades its refusal incomplete as it grades heti's other missing-evidence errors.
    The heti adapter's keri.process asks heti's key-state resolver about each message = decision:
      nid: 8uucj49z
      why: >-
        heti has no per-message KEL processor in its public API. Its public KEL verification surface is heti.anchored.consume.resolve_key_state(aid, evidence, as_of), a pure function that replays the evidence into a scratch keripy database with lax=False, drives keripy's escrows until a pass accepts nothing more, refuses an identifier with a fork anywhere in its delegation chain, and answers a key state or a coded error. So for keri.process the adapter makes each message one unattributed segment, in delivery order, and asks heti about the message's own event by its SAID (as_of=d): once with the messages delivered so far, for the initial reading, and once with all of them, for the final one. heti answering, or refusing only for duplicity (which it checks after it has found the event), means heti accepted the event: seen. A retryable refusal means heti could not establish it yet: pending. Any other refusal is rejected. A message's d is only what it claims, so when the messages delivered carry more than one body under one event's SAID, heti is asked about each body with the copies that carry another body left out: a corrupt copy claiming a valid event's SAID is then not reported seen because the valid copy was, while copies with the same body still combine their signatures as they would in heti. heti refuses all of the evidence when any segment does not begin with a genus/version code, so a message that does not, or that names no event, is offered to heti only in the question about itself, where heti's refusal is its reading, and never spoils the readings of the others. A request may carry at most 64 messages holding at most 1 MiB together, because every message costs two replays of up to all of them. Key states are heti's answer for each identifier the messages name, with all of them as evidence, and an identifier heti refuses to answer for is left out, which is heti's own position that a duplicitous identifier has no key state. heti exposes no reading of a receipt and no record of which events a superseding rotation displaced, so the adapter reports a non-key-event message as rejected, reports trunk equal to whether the message is finally seen, and declares neither keri.routing nor kel.recovery, so the cases that turn on those are not sent. heti refuses non-transferable identifiers by name, so kel.nontransferable is not declared either. The adapter reads only the i, s, d and t fields of a message body, to know what to ask; every verdict is heti's, and composes stays empty. Rejected calling keripy's Kevery directly from the adapter, which would test keripy rather than heti and which the item's brief forbids, and rejected opening a lockbox and resolving OOBIs as acdc.verify does, because add_other answers only for a whole KEL and would give no per-message reading at all. Tradeoff: two replays per message, cheap at case sizes but quadratic in the number of messages; and where heti adds nothing over keripy (the event checks themselves) the result is keripy's, which the README says.
